1BSD FROM SOURCE ON STOCK V6
============================

The directories here build the 1BSD software from its sources,
on a stock V6 system with the typesetter C toolchain from
../type_C installed.  The binaries on the 1BSD tape are not used.

As in type_C, all sources come unchanged from the 1BSD tape, and
each directory holds the ones it needs.  Where a change is
needed, run makes it with ed on a copy.

Each directory has two scripts:

	run	builds and tests, changes nothing outside
		this directory
	install	installs the result, after saving what it
		replaces

Run them as root, from their own directory, for example:

	chdir /usr/work/bsd1/tools
	sh run
	sh install


DIRECTORIES, IN THE ORDER THEY MUST BE INSTALLED

	ashell	the shell the 1BSD make scripts are written for
	tools	the 1BSD tools the make scripts use, and libX.a
	htmp	the terminal data bases, and sethome, ttytype, tset
	login	login and su that keep /etc/htmp up to date
	ex	the editor ex 1.1, with open and visual modes
	exrecover	expreserve and exrecover, which save and
		recover the buffers of ex


ashell

The make scripts on the tape use "set", "$*" and the alias "cd"
of ashell, so it comes first.  Put

	alias cd chdir

in .profile in your home directory; ashell has only chdir.

The sources come from the ashell directory of the tape.  run
fixes the uid in a copy of sh.c (see NOTES), compiles with
cc -f -n -O and -lS (calloc, strcpy, strcat and strcmp come from
libS), and runs ashtest with the new ashell.  It must print

	done

and nothing else.  ashtest checks the arguments, shift, rewind,
set arithmetic, ${name?a:b}, a goto loop with the V6 goto,
redirection, pipes, file name expansion, alias, and chdir with
and without an argument.  The checks of -c and of a script run by
name need /usr/bin/ashell, so they are skipped until install.

install saves an existing /usr/bin/ashell in save, installs
ashell there (that path is built into ashell, as the shell for
scripts), and runs ashtest again, now with all the checks.
To undo: cp save/ashell /usr/bin/ashell, or remove it.

Things to try by hand, as they are not easy to check in a script:
- no 15 second pause when ashell starts (see NOTES)
- "time ls -l /bin" prints a line like "0.3u 1.2s 0:02 75%",
  which uses the floating point simulated by fptrap
- "sleep 20 &" prints the process number and sets pcs; "wait"
  can be stopped with an interrupt
- ">*file" and "|*" also send the diagnostic output


tools

The sources come from s6 and s7 of the tape, and access.c is
new.  run builds, from the s6 sources,

	mkstr		puts error messages into a message file
	lnall		links files into a directory
	mvall		moves files into a directory
	cpall		copies files into a directory
	squash		strips object files with ld -r
	chownall	changes owners in a tree
	cptree		copies a tree, keeping links
	rmtree		removes a tree

and libX.a from the s7 sources (typeof, ttycap, ttycap2, htmpg,
htmps), with each member before the members it uses.  Ttyn.c of
s7 is not here: its table is made for the Berkeley /dev.

It then tests them in the directory t, and removes t with the new
rmtree.  Nothing may be printed between "any output between here
and done means failure" and "done".  Lines printed by ed are
normal.

install saves /usr/bin/rmtree (the shell script from
../vanilla/trees, if there is one) and /lib/libX.a, if there is
one, in save, and installs the tools in /usr/bin and libX.a in
/lib.  Programs built later link it with -lX, not with the
../lib/libX.a the tape scripts name.
To undo: copy save/rmtree to /usr/bin and save/libX.a to /lib,
or remove what was installed.

If ../vanilla/trees put a cptree in /bin, it is found before the
one in /usr/bin; install prints a note.  The 1BSD scripts only
call it as "cptree from to", which both accept.


htmp

ex and ashell learn the home directory and the terminal type from
three data bases:

	/etc/htmp	for each terminal, the uid, home directory
			and terminal type of its user
	/etc/ttytype	the type of each terminal, when it is not set
			in htmp
	/etc/ttycap	the capabilities of each type

Without them ex takes every terminal to be a model 33 teletype,
with no open or visual mode.

The sources are sethome.c, ttytype.c and tset.c from s6, and
ttycap from etc, all unchanged.  ttytype.v6 is new: the ttytype
in etc is the one for the Berkeley terminals.  It has one line,
8vt, for the console /dev/tty8.  Add a line for each other
terminal before install, as described in the file.

run compiles the three programs with -lX, so ../tools must be
installed first, and makes xttycap: the ttycap of the tape with a
new entry vt (vt100, ansi) for the terminal emulators of today.
Its modes are 0:0, so that tset leaves the tty modes alone.  It
has "ca" (cursor addressing), which ../ex adds to ex for vt.  It
has no "am": a vt100 waits at the last column before it wraps,
and ex 1.1 expects a terminal with "am" to wrap at once.

install saves what it replaces in save, installs xttycap as
/etc/ttycap and ttytype.v6 as /etc/ttytype, makes an empty
/etc/htmp owned by bin (an existing one is kept), and installs
the programs in /usr/bin, set user id bin so that they can write
/etc/htmp.  It must be run on a terminal.  It then sets the type
of the terminal to vt and checks it, and checks that sethome
gives "/" for root.  Nothing may be printed between "any output
between here and done means failure" and "done".  Last it lists
the types in /etc/ttycap.
To undo: copy back what is in save (ttytype.b is the program
/usr/bin/ttytype), and remove /etc/htmp.

Use:

	sethome [ dir ]		show or set your home directory in
				htmp (root cannot set it)
	ttytype [ type ]	show or set the type of this terminal;
				-n lists the types
	tset -h			sets the erase and kill characters and
				the modes for the type in htmp

Until login keeps htmp up to date, run "sethome dir" after logging
in as a user other than root.  The terminal type stays in htmp
for the terminal, so "ttytype vt" is needed only once.

tset sets the erase character to backspace (control-H) for a type
with "bs", vt included, unless it is given with -eC.  On a terminal
that sends DEL for its backspace key, it is better not to use it.
The other entries of the tape have modes like 0177440:0000332,
made for the V6 tty driver; with the driver in ../vanilla/tty
those bits mean other things, so do not use tset with them.


login

The sources are login.c and su.c from s8, unchanged.  When you
log in, login writes your uid, home directory and the type of the
terminal from /etc/ttytype into /etc/htmp, so sethome is no
longer needed.  It also prints .reminder in your home directory
(and in each directory on the way to it), runs .start_up there
with /bin/sh, and prints "Last login" if /usr/adm/lastlog exists.
su keeps htmp up to date as well, and puts the old entry back
when it exits.  Note that su changes to the home directory of the
new user.

They were written for the Berkeley system, so run makes these
changes on copies:

login
- alarm is not in V6: the two calls are removed (the V6 login
  has no time limit either)
- the passwords are read from /etc/passwd, not /etc/passwdf
- the utmp slot is 0-9 then a-z, as in the V6 login and init,
  not the terminal letter itself
- root may log in on any terminal, as with the V6 login (it was
  limited to the Berkeley terminals 5, 8 and t)
- V6 has separate uid and gid, where Berkeley used gid << 8 | uid
  as one id: login now calls setgid and setuid, and the tty and
  htmp get the uid only, which is what sethome and ex expect
- a missing /etc/kickoff exits with 0: before, the exit status
  was whatever was in r0, and login said "priority too low to log
  in" to everybody but root

su
- the passwords are read from /etc/passwd, not /etc/passwdf
- setgid and setuid, and the uid only in htmp, as for login
- the utmp slot as in login

run compiles them with -lX, so ../tools and ../htmp must be
installed first.  It does not install anything.

Before install, try them, as root on the console:

	./login yourname

logs you in, in a new shell under the root shell; check that
sethome gives your home directory, ashell starts without a pause,
and "ls -l /dev/tty8" shows you as the owner.  Log out with
control-d, which brings back the root shell, then run

	./su yourname

and check sethome again; control-d brings back root, and sethome
then gives what it gave before su.  The test logins leave your
entry in htmp and your name in utmp for the console, until the
next real login there; chown root /dev/tty8 to give the console
back to root.

install saves /bin/login and /bin/su in save, and installs the
new ones, set user id root.  Back up the disk images first: if
/bin/login does not work, nobody can log in.
To undo: cp save/login /bin/login; cp save/su /bin/su


ex

The sources are those of ex-1.1 on the tape, unchanged: the C
files and headers, the assembler files crt0.s (a startup that
leaves room for the arguments of "next"), printf.s (a printf that
prints through putchar), putchar.s, reset.s and wait.s, Version.c,
and the help files in how_ex.  ctest.c is new.

run builds the version for a machine without separate I and D,
like a.outNOID on the tape, as makeex does:

- mkstr moves the error messages into ex1.1strings and writes the
  sources into temp, where they are compiled, so ../tools must be
  installed
- ed adds cursor addressing for vt (ESC [ line ; column H) to the
  copy of ex_ca.c in temp: ex 1.1 only knows the ADM-3A, and the
  HP 2645 in the UNIMP version
- the C files are compiled without UNIMP: the visual operations
  e, E, < and > are left out, as on the 11/40 at Berkeley
- the objects are linked straight, not through the archive lib of
  makeex, with libX.a and the C library, and without the -n (pure
  text) of makeex: see NOTES
- Version.c of the tape is used: the script version that makes it
  runs ex itself

It then runs ctest, which checks that 'vt' is packed the way
ex_tty.c makes the terminal type, and edits a file with ex from
a command file.  Nothing may be printed between "any output
between here and done means failure" and "done".  run prints
the size of ex; "too large" from the test means it does not fit,
see NOTES.

install saves an existing /usr/bin/ex and /usr/lib/ex1.1strings
in save, and installs

	/usr/bin/ex		with /usr/bin/edit linked to it
	/usr/lib/ex1.1strings	the error messages
	/usr/lib/how_ex		the files of the help command

and runs the test again with /usr/bin/ex.
To undo: remove them, or copy back what is in save.

The "ca" in the vt entry of /etc/ttycap comes from ../htmp: if
that was installed before ex, run sh run and sh install in
../htmp again.  Then try ex by hand on the console:

	ex file		"vi" enters visual mode, "q" leaves it
	edit file	the simpler mode of ex for beginners
	help		in ex, uses /usr/lib/how_ex

A bad command must give a message, not just "ERROR"; that one
means /usr/lib/ex1.1strings is missing.  ex needs exrecover and
expreserve in /usr/lib for the recover command and after crashes;
they are the next step.


exrecover

The sources are expreserve.c and exrecover.c from the exrecover
directory of the tape, unchanged.

	expreserve	copies the buffer of ex into /usr/preserve:
			ex runs it for "preserve" and on a hangup,
			and "expreserve -a" saves all the buffers left
			in /tmp after a crash and mails their owners
	exrecover	finds a buffer in /tmp or /usr/preserve and
			gives it back: ex runs it for "recover file"
			and for "ex -r file"

run compiles expreserve, where copyout removes the file cp after
an error; cp is never set, so ed makes it pattern, the file being
written.  It is compiled without -O: the c2 of type_C dies on it from a
signal, which cc reports as "Fatal error in /lib/c2".  The cause
is not known yet.  It compiles exrecover with ex_io.c and ex_pack.c, which
it copies from ../ex with the headers they need, as makerecover
does: these are the plain sources, not the ones from mkstr, so the
messages of ex_io.c are printed by the error of exrecover.

install saves what it replaces in save, installs both in /usr/lib,
set user id root, and makes /usr/preserve, owned by root, mode
700.  It then preserves a buffer with ex, recovers it with another
ex, writes it to a file and compares it.  Nothing may be printed
between "any output between here and done means failure" and
"done"; ls of /usr/preserve prints nothing when exrecover has
removed the buffer it gave back, so buffers saved there before
show up too.
To undo: remove them, or copy back what is in save.

If /etc/rc removes the files in /tmp, put

	/usr/lib/expreserve -a

before that, so that the buffers of a crash are saved first.  If
it leaves /tmp alone, as the one of the V6 tape does, the line is
not needed: exrecover looks in /tmp first.


NOTES

- sethome checks that you are the user on the terminal: the
  owner of /dev/ttyX must be you, with group 0, which is what the
  V6 login does.  Otherwise it looks in /etc/utmp at slot X,
  which on V6 is the wrong slot (V6 puts 0-9 then a-z in order),
  and says "NOT super-user".  This only happens after su.

- su writes the reason and, for a wrong password, the password
  itself to /usr/adm/su, if that file exists.  Do not create it.

- The V6 getuid returns the real uid in the low byte and the
  effective uid in the high byte.  ashell keeps all 16 bits, and
  xid makes gid << 8 | uid from /etc/passwd, so the two never
  match: gethome fails, sleeps 15 seconds, fails again, and home
  becomes ".".  run masks the uid to 8 bits and makes xid return
  the uid only.

- ${name:default} gives "Undefined variable" when name is not
  set: in dolvbl the ':' case falls through to the check for an
  unset variable.  No 1BSD script uses it, so it is left alone.

- V6 has no access system call.  cptree and rmtree use it, so
  access.c does it with stat, for the real user and group ids.

- cpall and mvall call execl without the 0 that ends the
  argument list; run adds it.

- warning in rmtree returns no value when it does not ask (the
  -f flag, or input that is not a terminal), so what rmtree does
  then depends on what is left in r0.  run makes it return 1, go
  ahead.

- On an 11/40, V6 gives a process whole 8K pages, 8 in all: for a
  pure program the text, the data with bss, and the stack each
  take whole pages; for a program that is not pure, the text,
  data and bss together, and the stack.  The typesetter C makes
  ex 41088+3844+5036: pure, that is 6+2+1 pages, too large (the
  text is 128 bytes over 5 pages), and not pure 7+1, which fits.
  Either way about 7K is left for the line pointers of the
  buffer, which ex gets with sbrk.

- mkstr builds an octal escape with a shift of 7 instead of 3.
  None of the error messages in ex, pi or pxp use one, so it is
  left alone.
